I hope in this hypothetical case Susan gives Alex an earful in response. The markets are near ATH. If THIS isn't "good" time, when exactly would you want them to issue equity?

Capital is going to be VERY important in this race. Issuing equity at the right time and optimizing for long-term value of the business is much more important than enduring a random ~10-20% drawdown.

@SupBagholder I'm curious would you actually like it as a long term shareholder if they were thinking like this?

@RihardJarc well, they don't have to SPEND that money right away. They can raise cash and wait for the component costs to come down in the coming months. 

Do you think Meta would remain at &gt;$700 if they don't issue equity but S&amp;P 500 goes down 20% here? I don't think it will.

@SupBagholder they have been raising debt, and a good chunk of it is in off balance sheet. Having $100 Bn equity infusion would probably be healthy here to buy them some time to get back to debt market. They probably need to play a good tango here to do both.

lots of moving pieces here. If inflation/oil price starts to have a serious dent in consumer, core ad biz itself can face some issues. Hard to think in ceteris paribus. Anyways, as a shareholder I wouldn't want a company I own to optimize for decisions to save themselves from 10-20% drawdown in the short term. Meta is far from expensive, but it's also not as cheap as it was in couple of months ago when the news of equity raise first broke. We cannot cry for help no matter when they think of raising equity. I would have complained too if they raised equity below $600, but at $700-750, I think the option value of having additional cash can be more compelling.  

And all the AI researchers are rich enough to not worry about 10-20% drawdown in their stocks.

@SupBagholder a recent Meta backed debt was issued at 7.5%. Not exactly very cheap source of financing. And given the wide range of outcome for AIs, I would be more uncomfortable to saddle the balance sheet with noticeable debt if I owned the entire company.
